{
  "raw": {
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Professor_R0011012": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nThe Department of the Global Humanities at Suffolk University invites applications for a full-time, tenure track position at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning July 1, 2027 in the College of Arts & Sciences (CAS). We seek a teacher-scholar with research focus in Global History, broadly defined. Applicants should be able to teach and lead initiatives related to one or more of the following topic areas: Africa; Europe and European empires; Latin America; the Middle East; and/or Russia/the Soviet Union. We are looking for candidates who can expand the Department’s history offerings beyond its current strengths in East Asian history and twentieth-century U.S. history. We especially welcome candidates who could teach courses and lead initiatives in our Public History concentration for the History major ;   in other opportunities associated with the Department of the Global Humanities (e.g. with the Global Cultural Studies , Language, and/or Philosophy   programs ) ;   and   in   the College of Arts and Sciences ( e.g.   Environmental Studies,   Economics, Political Science/ Legal Studies ,   Black Studies, Economics,   and/or   Women’s and Gender Studies) .   P ublic history opportunities could include planning and leading Travel Seminars and Study Abroad/Away experiences and contributing to the already-established Bialystok Cemetery Restoration project. We are particularly interested in candidates who bring relevant experience and expertise that   could   prepare them to cultivate a   possible   partnership with the new Holocaust Museum & Education Center (set to open in Boston in 2027) and/or to plan new public history, experiential learning, and   service learning   initiatives in the greater Boston area and beyond to create meaningful experiences and professional development opportunities for our students.     Candidates should provide evidence of commitment to excellent teaching and scholarly potential, including a research agenda and plans for peer-reviewed publication. Candidates should be broadly trained to teach courses across the Department curriculum in research methods, professional skill building, and at the introductory, upper, and capstone levels including courses supporting the University’s first year and general education program. The successful candidate will teach courses that support the History major and minor, including the Global History concentration and the Public History concentration, as well as the Global Cultural Studies major and minor. The   candidate should be ready to contribute to a multidisciplinary department alongside students and faculty colleagues in History as well as in Language s , Global Cultural Studies, and Philosophy. In addition to   demonstrating   excellence in teaching and research, the successful candidate is expected to advise and mentor undergraduates from a variety of disciplines and fields of interest within the Department, and engage in service with the Department,   College   and University. The workload for this position is 45% teaching, 35% research, and 20% service with a 5-course teaching load per year.   Candidates must hold a PhD in History or a relevant field ( e.g.   Environmental   Studies;   Black Studies; Global Studies; Latin American Studies; Studies of Women, Gender, & Sexuality; or   another relevant   field) by the beginning of the 2027-2028 academic year. ABD candidates in the final stages of completing their degrees will be considered. Experience   teaching   undergraduate students preferred. Application should be made through the Suffolk University Human   Resources’   Careers at   Suffolk   web site.   A complete submission will   include:   cover letter; CV; Teaching Philosophy; Diversity Statement; a statement of research interests and agenda; student course evaluations (if available); a sample syllabus; and 3 letters of reference. Please have letters of recommendation sent by email to humanresources@suffolk.edu . For full consideration, applications should be   submitted   by October 15, though applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Initial interviews will be via Zoom followed by on-campus interviews.      The salary range for this role is $80,000 - $90,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Clinical-Professor---Director-of-Environmental-Law---Policy-Clinic_R0011013": {
      "title": "Clinical Professor – Director of Environmental Law & Policy Clinic",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University Law School welcomes applications for an entry-level tenure-track Clinical Professor to direct its Environmental Law and Policy Clinic. The Environmental Law & Policy Clinic (“ELPC”) was established in 2023 to meet growing student demand and urgent community need, and is one of Suffolk’s 15 year-long, faculty directed clinics. Suffolk Law’s Clinics are ranked in the top 5 of such programs nationally by U.S. News & World Report and have been ranked among the top 20 for more than a decade. The Clinical Professor will lead and teach all aspects of the ELPC, including managing the docket, supervising clinical students in their case and project work, and teaching the related clinical seminar. In addition to directing the Clinic, the faculty member will be expected to teach one non-clinical course in a related field, contribute to the Law School and community, and produce scholarship. We seek candidates with strong academic records and a commitment to excellence in clinical teaching and scholarship. Prior experience in clinical education is preferred. At least five years of environmental law experience is required. Experience with legislative advocacy and/or environmental policy is strongly preferred. Applicants must be admitted or eligible for admission to the Massachusetts Bar within six months of beginning the position. The ELPC is a full-year clinic offered for 10 credits (2 credits of seminar and 3 credits of casework per semester) serving students in their last 2 years of law school. Students enrolled in the ELPC work on a variety of local, state, and/or federal environmental, energy, and land use legal matters raising today’s most pressing issues. The clinic represents and advises non-profits, citizen groups, and governmental agencies in environmental legal and regulatory matters. Students also provide litigation support, analysis and education on various environmental legislative and regulatory initiatives. A substantial focus of the ELPC’s work is on supporting underserved communities and advocating on matters concerning land conservation, sand mining, and water resource protection in Southeastern Massachusetts.  The ideal candidate will be a self-motivated individual who can help shape the Clinic docket and enrich the program by developing relationships within the legal community, the communities served, the University, the legal academy, and the Bar.  Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, a list of references, and a cover letter addressed to Professor Christina Miller and Darrell Mottley, Co-Chairs of the Clinical Committee. All applications must be made through Suffolk’s job posting website, https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/en-US/External .       The salary range for this role is $120,000 - $140,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Dispatcher_R0010899": {
      "title": "Dispatcher",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Dispatcher is often the first point of contact for the Suffolk University and Security Department.  Duties include, but are not limited to, receiving and transmitting emergency and non-emergency calls for the service via radio, answering telephone calls and text messages, disseminating sensitive law enforcement information, running CJIS and CORI queries. The Dispatcher monitors closed-circuit television, alarm systems, multiple radio frequencies, foot traffic, maintains paper and electronic records, makes entries on a computer aided dispatching system. The Dispatcher must exhibit professional standards and provide excellent customer service in all interactions. The Dispatcher wears a Department-issued uniform and must follow the rules and regulations of the Department and the University. The Dispatcher must pass a proficiency test at the completion of their training period. The Dispatcher gives verbal directives to students, faculty, staff and visitors to ensure compliance with University policies and procedures with respect to public safety matters.   The Dispatcher answers telephones and aids callers and visitors. Administer First Aid and CPR to level of training. Requirements/Qualifications: A Dispatcher must demonstrate and maintain good judgment with the ability to independently respond and act quickly, accurately evaluate information and situations, and make appropriate decisions in routine and emergency situations. Ability to maintain self-control, poise, composure, and concentration while working in a fast-paced, multi-tasking environment with numerous interruptions and noise, and be able to remain calm, think clearly and use sound judgment when working with high pressure and stressful emergency situations. Knowledge of accepted office procedures, practices, and work methods, including strong record-keeping and prioritization skills; ability to type and operate general office and computerized equipment; ability to read, write, and spell at a level sufficient for satisfactory job performance. Possess excellent communication skills; speak clearly and concisely, follow oral and written instructions, transfer information accurately, and handle a wide range of interpersonal interactions effectively. Ability to learn standard broadcasting procedures, FCC rules regarding law enforcement radio transmission, and the 10-code system. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships, interact with all members of the campus community and public. Ability to maintain confidentiality of sensitive information. Ability to successfully complete all training, including appropriate basic and advanced Dispatcher training courses within the probationary period as required by the Department. Ability to comply with all Departmental uniform and equipment standards. The Dispatcher must be certified and maintain certification for access to the criminal justice information system [CJIS]. This will include the completion and passing of a written exam, hands on applications, fingerprints, and a criminal record check. The Dispatcher must attend required training programs, including but not limited to CPR, basic First Aid and AED applications. The Dispatcher must possess excellent written and verbal skills, apply written and verbal skills while working under stress, have the ability to apply excellent verbal and written skills for long periods of time, be assigned to a Dispatch Center for eight hours or more, work any and all shifts, work overtime and understand that they may be required to work at the direction of the shift supervisor. Other Employment Requirements: Must have a high school diploma or GED. Must be 18 years of age. Must be available to work any shift as assigned. Must have excellent communication and human relation skills. Preferably, have valid CPR and First Aid certifications. Must have no prior felony record (for certification access into CJIS) The University will conduct a background check which will include a criminal record check.  A pre-employment physical exam, drug screening, and fingerprints are required.  The University will conduct a verification of previous employment and reference checks. Physical Activities: Sitting and standing for extended periods of time. Walking and running as needed. Walking up and down stairs. Ability to utilize human senses, sight, sound, touch, taste, smell. Physically detaining a human being under the direction of a police officer or supervisor Lifting a human as needed Pulling/pushing a human being Work Environment: •     Ability to work report to work during all types of weather. •     Ability to work flexible hours including all rotating shift assignments, including nights weekends, and holidays. Be available for on-call assignment and overtime as needed. •     Ability to sit or stand at a location for extended periods of time. •     Ability to walk for extended periods of time.     The salary range for this role is $23.00 -$26.00 depending upon experience.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track---Undergraduate-Law_R0011027": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure Track), Undergraduate Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Department of Political Science & Legal Studies in the College of Arts & Sciences at Suffolk University invites applications for a full-time tenure track Assistant Professor position in our Undergraduate Law program beginning July 1, 2027. We are seeking a teacher-scholar with a teaching and research program focused on equity and inclusion in the law that may include a specialization in contracts, family law, domestic violence, employment law, intellectual property, legal research and writing, immigration law, or related fields of the law. Candidates should provide evidence of commitment to excellent teaching and scholarly potential. The Undergraduate Law Program prepares students for law school and careers as non-lawyer legal professionals. The Department includes undergraduate majors in Political Science, International Relations and Undergraduate Law, a Paralegal Studies Certificate and Associate Degree Program (for Non-Lawyer Legal Professionals) as well as Master of Arts degrees in Applied Public Policy and Global Public Policy. Preference will be given to those who have experience teaching legal research and writing and working with paralegals and/or supervising legal staff. Candidates should be able to contribute to the college’s core curriculum. In addition to demonstrating excellence in teaching and research, the successful candidate is expected to advise and mentor undergraduate students, and engage in service with the department, college and university. The workload for this position is 45% teaching, 35% research, and 20% service with a 5-course teaching load per year. Suffolk’s Political Science & Legal Studies Department’s faculty are teacher scholars with a strong commitment to student development by providing high quality instruction to a diverse student body. Faculty in the department are award-winning teachers recognized for innovative pedagogical practices, community leaders civically engaged in advancing social justice, internationally recognized policy and political consultants and active researchers and scholars. Initiatives such as Suffolk Votes, the non-partisan university-wide initiative to raise political awareness and impact by increasing student voter participation, reflect the department’s key role in Suffolk’s Community Engagement Carnegie Classification. Department faculty take advantage of our location in the heart of Boston to design exciting real-world learning opportunities at the Court House and the State House. Close connections with Suffolk Law School, which has top-ranked skills clinics, provide students in the Undergraduate Law program with rich pre-professional opportunities as does our thriving Center for Restorative Justice. The successful candidate will contribute to a rich tradition of community-engaged teaching and experiential education that prepares our diverse, substantially first-generation college students—40% of whom are Pell eligible—for careers and civic engagement. The successful candidate will have a commitment to inclusive pedagogy and curricular diversity, mentoring and advising students—especially first-generation college students—as well as conducting impactful research. We seek candidates excited about centering best practices pedagogy focused on active, applied and experiential learning in their teaching; a commitment to ongoing and continuous improvement in teaching is expected. Familiarity with and experience using pedagogical methods that enable students across racial, ethnic, socio-economic, and ability groups to reach their maximum potential is a valuable qualification. Candidates must hold a JD (combination JD and PhD in a relevant field also invited) by July 2027. Experience teaching undergraduates is preferred. Completed submissions must include: cover letter; CV; law school transcript; statement of Teaching Philosophy; Diversity Statement; a statement of research interests and agenda; if the candidate has teaching experience, the last two active semesters of student course evaluations; and three names of reference. For full consideration, applications should be submitted by November 1, 2026, though applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Initial interviews will be via Zoom followed by on-campus interviews. Not sure this is you? Contact us to find out! Research indicates that women and members of marginalized populations tend not to apply to jobs when they think they do not meet the qualifications, when, in fact, they often do. Learn more about CAS faculty here . To learn more about the Political Science & Legal Studies Department visit our website . Inquiries about the position should be addressed to Search Chair Stephanie Jirard, Political Science & Legal Studies Department, at stephanie.jirard@suffolk.edu.     The salary range for this role is $95,000 - $105,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Seasonal-Recruiter-Position_R0011057": {
      "title": "Seasonal Recruiter Position",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Suffolk University Law School Office of Admissions is seeking an enthusiastic and professional  Seasonal Recruiter to assist with fall recruitment efforts. The Seasonal Recruiter will serve as an ambassador for Suffolk University Law School at law school recruitment events across the country and will play an important role in engaging prospective students and positively representing the law school. The ideal candidate will be outgoing, organized, detail-oriented, professional, efficient, and comfortable interacting with a wide range of prospective students and other event participants. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, as well as a genuine desire to represent Suffolk University Law School, are essential. Key Responsibilities The Seasonal Recruiter will: Attend law school fairs, forums, and other recruitment events throughout the country. Represent Suffolk University Law School in a positive, professional, and enthusiastic manner. Answer prospective students’ questions about Suffolk University Law School, the admissions process, and the student experience. Distribute admissions materials and other recruitment information. Research and identify appropriate travel arrangements for assigned recruiting events. Maintain organized records and notes regarding recruitment trips and interactions with prospective students. Provide thoughtful assessments and feedback following recruiting events to assist the Office of Admissions in planning future recruitment efforts. Complete other recruitment-related duties as assigned. Assist in the Law Admission Office when not on recruiting trips. Qualifications and Requirements Candidates must: Be a Suffolk University Law School alum. Have graduated from Suffolk University Law School in good academic and disciplinary standing. Possess strong interpersonal and communication skills. Be organized, detail-oriented, professional, outgoing, and efficient. Demonstrate a strong desire to positively represent Suffolk University Law School. Possess a valid driver’s license. Possess a major credit card for approved travel-related arrangements and expenses. Schedule and Time Off This is a temporary position running from September through November 2026. Because recruitment events will take place throughout the fall and will require travel, scheduling will vary based on the recruitment calendar and assigned events. Days off will be determined on a case-by-case basis. Compensation and Travel The Seasonal Recruiter will be compensated on a per diem basis. The salary for this position is $51,000 annually, prorated to the amount of time worked. All approved travel expenses incurred in connection with work-related recruiting activities will be reimbursed in accordance with Suffolk University Law School procedures. Cover Letter Requirement Interested candidates should submit a cover letter explaining their interest in serving as a Seasonal Recruiter, their connection to Suffolk University Law School, and how their experience and skills would contribute to the Office of Admissions’ fall recruitment efforts.     The salary range for this role is $51,000.00.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Professor-of-Academic-Support_R0011052": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Academic Support",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 9 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University Law School in Boston invites applications for two full-time positions as Assistant Professor of Academic Support beginning in the 2027–2028 academic year. The Academic Support Program (ASP) at Suffolk is an integral part of the Law School faculty and is led by professors who have received national recognition for their work in academic support and legal education. ASP professors collaborate closely with the academic deans, the Dean of Students Office, and faculty colleagues to promote student learning throughout law school and on the bar exam. ASP faculty appointments are not tenure track, but ASP professors have a path to long-term, presumptively renewable contracts and receive the same research support resources available to tenure track faculty. The responsibilities of ASP faculty at Suffolk include working individually with students throughout the year to identify areas for improvement and develop the study and analytical skills necessary for success in law school and on the bar exam. ASP professors also teach an upper-level course on legal analysis and/or bar examination skills and may have opportunities to teach additional electives related to their areas of expertise or research interests. ASP faculty teach and work with students in both the day and evening divisions, both in person and online. Applications shoud be submitted through Suffolk’s hiring portal and include a curriculum vitae and letter of interest. The letter should be addressed to Professor Sarah Schendel, Director of the Academic Support Program and Chair of the Academic Success Committee. Questions may be directed to Professor Schendel at sschendel@suffolk.edu . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the positions are filled. For fullest consideration, applicants should submit their materials by September 25, 2026.     The salary range for this role is $85,000 - $100,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Campus-Safety-and-Resource-Officer_R0010839-1": {
      "title": "Campus Safety and Resource Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-21\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Campus Safety and Resource Officer is the first line ambassador to the community, providing various services, offering assistance and direction and ensuring that campus facilities are safe and secure by controlling access, documenting those who enter and monitoring, responding to emergencies and reporting all security, safety and operational concerns.  Primary/Principal Responsibilities: Reporting to the shift supervisor, the duties of the Campus Safety and Resource Officer include but are not limited to: Acting as a first-line representative and ambassador of the university when encountering and greeting members of the university community including students, faculty, staff, alumni, invited guests, vendors and members of the general public.  This includes: Greeting each person who enters a university facility in a friendly and welcoming manner. Providing high-touch, support and assistance to community members including: Providing directions and relevant information to assist community members and guests with wayfinding. Providing general university information including operating hours, contact information, and other general university information to the community as provided through training and directive. Being aware of the events of the day and assisting and directing attendees and others who inquire. Assisting community members and guests with accessing the services of other departments as needed by contacting other offices, communicating on community members behalf and/or directing them to the correct resource. Serving as a friendly and helpful resource to the community by answering telephones and providing assistance to callers and visitors with various needs, such as: Documenting requests for assistance to be handled by the Campus Safety and Resource Team or another campus partner and, relaying to the shift supervisor to ensure the appropriate response. Providing information including campus access hours, library hours, assistance with locating and connecting with specific departments or events, etc.  Monitoring, facilitating and providing access control for the entry and exit of individuals who have business on campus to ensure campus safety by: Requesting that all university community members present and, whenever available, electronically swipe/tap their University identification each time they enter a facility to ensure that permission to enter is granted. Prior to allowing entry into any facility, verifying that the identification presented by each individual entering a facility is that of the bearer and that, according to prescribed procedures, the individual is not prohibited or restricted from entry for any reason. When appropriate, notifying the supervisor of the entry of an individual who is flagged but not banned.  Checking-in university guests (those who do not have university identification cards), who have relevant business to conduct in the facility according to a prescribed process to ensure that all those who enter a campus facility are documented and that no person who is identified as prohibited or restricted is allowed into a university facility. When asked, providing individuals who are denied entry due to prohibition or restriction with contact information to inquire about their status. Providing assistance to those who require information and/or direction in locating specific university departments, individuals or campus events by verbally providing directions, escorting individuals to the appropriate location or providing printed and/or electronic resources that will assist in locating the desired location/individual. Observing the activities of those in and around campus facilities, in person and via CCTV monitors, and promptly reporting to the shift supervisor, any unsafe, illegal or nefarious activity. Providing support to the campus community by acting as a liaison in support of campus operations, including: Observing, recognizing and promptly reporting to the shift supervisor any facilities related concerns and challenges such as leaks, trip and fall hazards or other dangerous conditions, damage to university property, alarms, graffiti, excessive heat or cold, obviously malfunctioning equipment, etc. Assisting the community by welcoming reports of facilities related concerns and challenges such as those mentioned above and others from members of the university community and promptly relaying them to the shift supervisor. Through the shift supervisor, requesting assistance from the University Police Department for those who require assistance with the following: Lost and Found Safety escorts to the MBTA, local parking facilities or other university facilities And other police related inquiries When directed by the shift supervisor, responding to calls for service such as: Lockouts Unlocking/locking doors at the beginning/end of the day, turning on/off lights, opening/closing shades Investigation of facilities and other operational related issues and emergencies for the purpose of confirming reported conditions and/or providing updates to other campus partners Unlocking/locking and opening/closing exterior doors to facilitate deliveries, etc.  Responding to reports of injuries, medical and mental health emergencies to confirm the presence of an emergency, evaluate additional resources needed and render aid Requests for assistance in support of campus operations including picking up/hand delivering items, triaging needs, reporting needs to the appropriate campus resources and observing/reporting site conditions to assist offsite resources with appropriate response. When a fire alarm is activated, a medical emergency occurs or other emergent condition exists, the officer will support the response of the University Police, Boston Fire Department, Emergency Medical Services, the Boston Police or other relevant agency by providing and assisting with access, providing relevant information and by facilitating access to other campus personnel and resources. Providing first aid for injuries, medical and mental health emergencies within the standard of care commensurate with the officer’s level of training and promptly requesting emergency medical services response when appropriate. At the direction of the shift supervisor, executing interior and/or exterior inspections of campus facilities with or without utilizing a “Detex” tour system for the purpose of detecting security, facility, health/safety or other operational concerns, logging and reporting those, with the assistance/direction of the shift supervisor, to the appropriate party. Give verbal directives to students, faculty, staff and visitors to ensure compliance with University policies and procedures with respect to public safety matters. Verbally detain offenders of University public safety policies including: Those who attempt to enter a university facility who have been prohibited or restricted from entering a university facility. Those who are attempting to enter a university facility without providing identification.  Those who are visibly intoxicated/impaired and appear unable to safely care for themselves. Those who are attempting to enter a university facility while in the possession of prohibited items including alcohol, illicit drugs, weapons and other prohibited or items that are inherently dangerous such as fuel or implements designed to create an open flame. When verbal detention is ineffective, promptly requesting a response of the University Police and providing a prompt and thorough description of the individual and their direction of flight to the responding police officers. When necessary, officers will assist with evacuating campus facilities including providing direction to those leaving the facility, providing way finding to the appropriate campus evacuation assembly area, completing a head count, etc.  Providing residential students with assistance in the following areas: Contacting the Resident Assistant or Administrator-on-call when necessary, according to the prescribed procedure. Assisting students who have become locked out of their rooms. Assisting students who wish to report facilities concerns by investigating, documenting and notifying facilities through the work order system or, for emergent issues, by contacting the shift supervisor for further direction.  Monitoring Security Equipment Officers will monitor CCTV equipment provided at campus facilities to detect and mitigate unlawful/unauthorized entry, hazardous conditions, facility or operational emergencies and inefficiencies and officers will promptly report to the shift supervisor, any condition that requires further investigation or response by a campus partner. Officers will monitor campus facility fire alarm systems and will promptly notify the shift supervisor of any alarm condition. Creating University Identification Cards When necessary, officers will utilize the campus identification system to take ID photos and create university ID cards following the prescribed procedure. In the case of replacement cards, officers will document appropriately to ensure that appropriate charges are placed on the requestor’s university account. Documentation and Related Activities Officers will operate a personal computer only for authorized university business including using commercially available and proprietary software for recording and accessing documentation and policies and procedures, accessing university email, the student/employee information system (for verifying the identity of those who have forgotten their ID card) and accessing general campus information for the purpose of assisting community members. When required by the shift supervisor, the officers will complete an Incident Report per the prescribed procedure to document the information associated with a particular incident including what was reported to the officer, the actions of others observed by the officer and the actions taken by the officer(s) involved. Officers at all facility assignments will maintain an activity log that will be submitted to the supervisor at the end of the shift that shall include: Shift log-on time Documentation of all requests for assistance requiring a response by a member of the team or another campus partner including the University Police Documenting the response to all first aid or mental health first aid incidents.  Lunch period Documentation of all deliveries that are made through loading or service entrances. Documentation of all other incidents, exceptions or activities directed by the supervisor either on an ad hoc basis or by way of standing order. Shift log off time   Officers will complete relevant data entry and reporting based on prescribed procedures. Officers will take photographs as part of documenting an incident or general condition. Officers will be required to remain at and staff a specific assignment/location when not otherwise assigned or dispatched by the shift supervisor. Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Must have a high school diploma or GED. Must be 18 years of age. Must be available to work any shift as assigned as well as both voluntary and required overtime Must have excellent communication and human relation skills. Must have no prior felony record. Must have the ability to remain calm and task focused in challenging environments ranging from chaotic to mundane and tedious. Must have the ability to read, write and speak the English language at a sufficient level that: Allows the officer to converse fluently with individuals in a higher education setting, often during stressful, emergent situations, To read, understand and apply often complex information and procedures and To write clear, concise and precise reports, logs, memorandums and other related documentation in a manner that ensures accuracy in reporting and readability by others. Must be able to use a personal computer and able to learn and use both commercially available and proprietary software and complete various tasks using a computer including performing data entry, running reports/inquiries, and printing documents and identification cards. Capable of successfully completing various trainings provided by the university including a Massachusetts First Responder certification (under 105 CMR 171) and Mental Health First Aid certification, Title VI and Title IX training and maintaining these certifications as a condition of employment.  Preferably, have a valid American Heart Association CPR and First Aid certification. Physical activities include but are not limited to: Sitting and standing for extended periods of time Ability to walk for extended periods of times and run for the purpose of expediting response to a campus location. Walking up and down stairs Lifting a human as needed Pulling/pushing a human being Ability to utilize human senses, sight, sound, touch, taste, smell. Work environment: Ability to work inside and outside of buildings during all types of weather. Ability to work flexible hours including all rotating shift assignments, nights, weekends, holidays; available for on-call assignment and overtime as needed. Officers will be issued uniforms/equipment commensurate with their duties and are required to wear the uniform at all times while on duty as well as maintain all uniforms and equipment in a professional manner.  All uniforms and equipment are and remain the property of the university and must be returned to the university upon request and/or at separation.  Officers are not permitted to use personal electronic devices/phones/pagers/tablets, etc. while on-duty without express permission from the shift supervisor.  The officer will maintain confidentiality when entrusted with information relevant to individuals or situations and will not divulge this information to unauthorized third parties.  The officer may at times be entrusted with information that is protected by FERPA, HIPAA or other privileged information. The officer will comply with all relevant University directives when handling confidential and/or privileged information and will not disclose this information to anyone who is not expressly authorized. The officer will not make disclosures of any type to the media but will refer all inquiries from media organizations to an authorized spokesperson in the Public Affairs Office. Officers will follow established procedures as documented and published by the university.  These procedures are subject to constant review and update/change by department leadership to ensure operational and safety standards are maintained.  Officers will remain up to date on the latest procedures and will adjust their behavior as changes arise. Fraternization between officers and registered students is prohibited as a condition of employment.  Note: Security Officer’s do not have a warrant as a Special State Police Officer and thus have no arrest authority. Other: The use of tobacco products while on duty is prohibited. The University may slot a new hire at any step above the starting rate for his or her position, taking into account the individual’s qualifications and experience.     The salary range for this role is Security Officer Rates Start $21.16 1 Year $21.44 3 Year $22.00 5 Year $22.50 7 Year $23.00 10 Year $23.50 15 Year $24.00.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Graduate-Admissions-Counselor_R0011029": {
      "title": "Graduate Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Graduate Admission Counselor is the entry-level professional role on Suffolk University’s Graduate Admission team, and the foundation of the team’s recruitment and advising capacity. Reporting to the Associate Director of Graduate Admission, this position manages an assigned caseload of graduate programs across the College of Arts & Sciences (CAS) and the Sawyer Business School (SBS), guiding prospective students from first inquiry through enrollment decision. The Graduate Admission Counselor is a high-energy, student-centered role that combines travel and recruitment, applicant advising, file review, and relationship-building with program directors and campus partners. A particular area of emphasis for this position is supporting access and inclusion efforts — serving as the admission team’s primary liaison to the Center for Student Diversity & Inclusion, the Center for Academic Access and Opportunity, and community-based organizations focused on first-generation and historically underrepresented graduate student populations. This position is ideal for candidates who are passionate about graduate education, thrive in a collaborative team environment, and are eager to develop a broad foundation of admission skills that supports advancement toward the Assistant Director and Associate Director levels. A limited hybrid schedule is available based on departmental needs and the enrollment cycle. Recruitment & Territory Management Execute recruitment activities for an assigned portfolio of CAS and SBS graduate programs, with the goal of maximizing inquiry generation and applicant conversion. Represent Suffolk University at on- and off-campus events including graduate fairs, information sessions, community college visits, professional association events, and corporate partner sessions. Travel approximately 6–8 weeks per year during fall and spring recruitment cycles. Manage a defined prospect territory using Slate CRM, maintaining current and accurate data on outreach activity, pipeline status, and applicant progression. Advise prospective students through multi-channel communication — in-person, virtual, phone, email, and text — on program options, application requirements, and the enrollment process. Collaborate with program directors to stay current on program offerings and participate in faculty-led recruitment initiatives. Applicant Advising & File Review Manage the admissions process for an assigned caseload of graduate program applicants, maintaining a high standard of responsiveness and service from inquiry through enrollment decision. Follow up with applicants via email, phone, and CRM to ensure applications are complete and moving through the review process. Evaluate applications using established program criteria, assign preliminary decisions for Director review, and contribute to merit scholarship rankings as applicable. Maintain data integrity in Slate and contribute accurate, timely updates to enrollment funnel reports. Refer complex advising situations or policy questions to the Associate Director or Director as appropriate. Office Contributions & Professional Development Participate in office-wide projects, committee work, and initiatives as assigned by the Director or Associate Director. Support the planning and execution of recruiting events as directed, in collaboration with the Assistant Director who leads event logistics. Contribute to team training sessions, onboarding activities, and continuous improvement efforts. Actively pursue professional development opportunities to build expertise in graduate enrollment management and advance toward the Assistant Director level. Other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution Demonstrated interest in higher education, enrollment management, student services, or a related field Strong written and oral communication skills, including the ability to present professionally to diverse audiences Excellent interpersonal skills and a genuine commitment to helping students navigate the graduate admission process Ability to manage multiple priorities and meet frequent, time-sensitive deadlines in a fast-paced environment Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion — particularly in working with first-generation and historically underrepresented student populations Proficiency with Microsoft Office and comfort learning new technology platforms; CRM experience a plus Ability to travel approximately 8-10 weeks per year domestically and internationally for recruitment events Must be 18 years old, have at least two years of driving experience and have a valid driver’s license from the U.S. or U.S. Territories; must submit to an annual driver record check and maintain an acceptable driving record per university and/or insurance carrier requirements; must complete the University driver safety training within 60 days of hire and additional driver training as needed. Valid passport required for international travel Ability to work evenings and weekends as required by the recruitment cycle Preferred 1–3 years of experience in college admission, recruitment, sales, student services, or a related field Experience with Slate CRM or comparable admissions technology Familiarity with graduate-level programs in liberal arts, sciences, or business disciplines Experience working with or advocating for underrepresented student populations in an educational or community setting Bilingual candidate a plus     The salary range for this role is $45,000-$60,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Environmental-Law---Policy-Clinic---Clinical-Fellow_R0011023": {
      "title": "Environmental Law & Policy Clinic - Clinical Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 18 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University Law School’s top-10 ranked Clinical Programs welcomes applications for a Clinical Fellow to help teach and supervise students in its Environmental Law and Policy Clinic during the 2026-27 academic year. The Environmental Law & Policy Clinic (“ELPC”) was established in 2023 to meet growing student demand in the area of environmental law. It is one of 15 in-house clinics at Suffolk Law School. The Fellow will work with a Visiting Clinical Professor in the ELPC on all aspects the program, including managing the docket, supervising clinical students in their case and project work, and helping to teach the related clinical seminar. Clinical Fellows are staff-level junior clinical teachers and receive extensive mentoring and opportunities for professional development. The ELPC is a full-year clinic offered for 10 credits (2 credits of seminar and 3 credits of casework per semester) serving students in their last 2 years of law school. Students enrolled in the ELPC work on a variety of local, state, and/or federal environmental, energy, and land use legal matters, including representing and advising non-profits, citizen groups, legislators, and governmental agencies on regulatory and environmental policy issues. During the 2026-27 academic year, the ELPC will substantially focus its work on supporting underserved communities and advocates on matters concerning land conservation, sand mining, and resource protection in Southeastern Massachusetts. Students will also provide analysis and education on various legislative/regulatory initiatives. Requirements/Qualifications: Eligible candidates must possess a JD and be admitted to the Massachusetts Bar or qualified to be admitted within six months of the start date. Successful Clinical Fellow candidates will have at least 2-5 years of relevant legal experience in environmental, energy, land use, municipal or administrative law and/or legislative and regulatory drafting and advocacy. Experience with, or demonstrated interest in, legislative advocacy and/or environmental legal policy is strongly preferred. Experience and/or interest in mentoring and working with junior attorneys or law students is required. Past participation in a law school clinic is preferred but not required. Experience or interest in working with diverse communities, clients, and other stakeholders. Prior teaching experience is not required.  Hours and Duration:  This position will begin as soon as possible, and will be a full-time, 1-year appointment with the possibility of future renewal. Application: Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, a list of references, and a cover letter addressed to Professor Christina Miller, Chair of the Clinical Committee. All applications must be made through Suffolk’s job posting website, https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/en-US/External .     The salary range for this role is $70,000 - $80,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-Campus/Admissions-Counselor_R0011011": {
      "title": "Admissions Counselor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nReporting to the Senior Associate Director of Admission, this position is part of an admissions unit responsible for the recruitment and admission of undergraduate students. Through personalized outreach, thoughtful application review, and strategic recruitment efforts, this role helps shape an incoming class of students who will thrive at Suffolk University. The counselor will manage a recruitment territory, build meaningful relationships with students and community partners, and contribute to enrollment goals through a combination of travel, data-informed decision making, and student-centered service. Most importantly, you’d be joining a team that is positive, engaging, and committed to our shared goals. If you’re passionate about helping students, value the importance of our historical mission of access, opportunity, and inclusion; we welcome your application for this position. What you’ll accomplish in this role: Represent the university both virtually and in-person at college fairs, secondary schools, and community colleges (as needed). Travel and recruit approximately 10-12 weeks between the fall and spring semesters. Conduct virtual and on/off-campus interviews with students and families as requested. Manage a designated recruitment territory and develop strategic outreach plans to increase awareness, applications, and enrollment. Demonstrate understanding of Suffolk University’s mission and our commitment to diversity and inclusion. Think strategically about your assigned recruitment market and take a creative, data-driven approach to increase enrollment. Holistically and efficiently evaluate first-year applications. This includes issuing admission decisions and rankings for honors consideration or special merit scholarship programs. Counsel applicants, their families, school counselors, and advisors on Suffolk University’s admission policies and procedures. Coordinate and implement application generation and yield programs in assigned regions.  Assist the Welcome Center in conducting virtual and on-campus information sessions and programming for students and families. Will be assigned additional project and liaison work as needed. What would make you a successful and qualified candidate: A love of creativity and innovation with a willingness to try new approaches to achieve goals. Possess strong written and verbal communication skills with ability to speak publicly in front of small and large audiences. Being a relationship builder with the ability to engage with prospective applicants, families, transfer advisors, and secondary school counselors. The ability to successfully manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and projects while maintaining exceptional attention to detail. Exhibit excellent critical thinking and problem-solving skills. You are comfortable with both identifying challenges and providing recommendations for improvement. Proficiency with technology including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, CRM systems (preferably Slate), social media, Zoom, Google Meet, Microsoft Teams etc. Ability to work independently and as part of a team on group projects. A positive and team-focused approach to your work with a willingness to go the extra mile. What is required: Bachelor’s Degree Must have at least two years of driving experience and have a valid driver’s license from the U.S. or U.S. Territories . Must submit to an annual driver record check and maintain an acceptable driving record per university and/or insurance carrier requirements; must complete the University driver safety training within 60 days of hire and additional driver training as needed. This position requires the ability and comfort with traveling for recruitment in the fall and spring, often for extensive periods of time. This includes frequent driving in a variety of locations and occasional air travel. Ability and flexibility to work outside regular office hours with some evening and weekend work. This role is not fully remote and requires the ability to be in-office 4 days per week. Additional remote days are evaluated seasonally based on office coverage needs.     The salary range for this role is $45,000-$60,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Director-of-Orientation--New-Student---Family-Programs_R0011007": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Orientation, New Student & Family Programs",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-14\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPrimary Responsibilities Orientation & Transition Program Management Co-lead the planning, implementation, and assessment of comprehensive Orientation programs for new first-year and transfer students, including summer, fall, winter, and mid-year experiences. Assist with coordinate program logistics, schedules, student participation, campus partner involvement, and event execution to ensure a high-quality transition experience. Manage orientation registration processes, participation tracking, reporting, and attendee communications. Evaluate program effectiveness through assessment tools, surveys, and feedback mechanisms, utilizing data to recommend and implement program improvements. Support the development and delivery of transition programs that promote student engagement, belonging, persistence, and academic success. Assist with large-scale university events that support new student onboarding and transition efforts. Student Leadership Development & Supervision Provide direct supervision of office student staff. Assist in supervision of Orientation Team Leaders, Orientation Leaders, RAM Supporters, and other student staff supporting Orientation and transition initiatives. Coordinate student leader recruitment, selection, hiring, onboarding, training, scheduling, supervision, and evaluation processes. Manage the daily work assignments and professional development of office student employees, ensuring effective support for departmental operations, customer service, and program implementation. Develop and facilitate leadership development opportunities that promote communication, mentorship, inclusion, and student success. Provide ongoing coaching, mentorship, and performance feedback to student leaders. Foster a positive and inclusive student employment experience while maintaining high standards for professionalism and accountability. Support assessment efforts to measure student leader effectiveness and program outcomes. Communications & Student Engagement Coordinate and implement communication strategies that support students and families throughout the onboarding and transition process. Manage communication campaigns utilizing CRM systems, text messaging platforms, email communications, and web-based resources. Create and maintain content for orientation materials, websites, newsletters, presentations, and other student-facing resources. Respond to student and family inquiries, providing timely and accurate information regarding orientation, transition programs, and university resources. Collaborate with campus partners to ensure consistent messaging and coordinated outreach efforts. Support family engagement initiatives that educate families about the student transition experience and available campus resources . Campus Partnerships & Student Success Initiatives Collaborate with Enrollment Management on initiatives that support student yield, onboarding, enrollment, and retention efforts. Partner with academic and administrative departments to coordinate new student requirements, advising initiatives, placement processes, and specialized orientation programs. Work closely with departments including Academic Advising, Residence Life, Student Affairs, Athletics, International Student Services, and other campus partners to support successful student transitions. Assist in the planning and implementation of Family Weekend and other campus-wide engagement programs. Represent Orientation, New Student & Family Programs on divisional committees, working groups, and campus initiatives. Contribute to retention-focused initiatives and programs that support student success beyond Orientation. Operations & Administrative Coordination Manage databases, registration systems, student records, reports, and communications related to Orientation and transition programs. Maintain accurate program documentation, assessment records, and operational procedures. Assist with the development, management, and reconciliation of departmental budgets, including monitoring expenditures, processing purchases, and ensuring appropriate stewardship of resources Assist in the administration of vendor agreements and service contracts, including coordinating purchasing processes, maintaining documentation, and ensuring compliance with university policies and procedures. Monitor shared departmental communication channels and ensure timely follow-up. Support office operations and special projects as assigned. Additional duties may be assigned as needed to support departmental and divisional priorities. Requirements Bachelor's degree required; master’s degree preferred in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Counseling, Student Personnel Administration, or a related field. Two to four years of professional experience in orientation programs, student affairs, academic advising, student success, or a related higher education field preferred. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing, and assessing programs and initiatives. Experience supervising, advising, mentoring, or training student leaders. Strong organizational and project management skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities simultaneously. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to build collaborative relationships with students, families, faculty, staff, and external partners. Demonstrated commitment to supporting diverse student populations and fostering inclusive environments. Ability to exercise sound judgment, solve problems independently, and work effectively in a fast-paced environment. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and the ability to learn and utilize student information, CRM, and event management systems. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive information with discretion. Must be able to work occasional evenings and weekends to support Orientation programs and university events.     The salary range for this role is $60,000–$70,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Director-of-the-University-Honors-Program_R0011003": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of the University Honors Program",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University is pleased to announce a career opportunity as the Assistant Director of the University Honors Program!  If you enjoy working with students, managing events and programs as well as making a positive impact on students as they pursue their undergraduate academic programs, this career opportunity is for you!  Suffolk offers free tuition as part of a generous benefits package. You may be interested in pursuing your master's degree or taking classes for free! Please submit a cover letter along with your resume to be considered for this career opportunity. This role serves the mission of Suffolk University and supports the undergraduate honors program. The Assistant Director reports to the University Honors Director and manages and motivates student groups; produces honors program materials and is responsible for social media; creates and oversees an active calendar of programs and events, budget planning, and is  involved with  scheduling and recruiting and retention. The Assistant Director also serves as the primary support on honors issues and as deputy to the University Honors Director, liaising with other campus offices, external partners, families, and alumni. Primary/Principal Responsibilities:   Program Management Collaborate with the University Honors Director and other office staff regarding program management Create and execute administrative processes and special projects Create, automate, and update processes and resources to create efficiencies or meet evolving program needs Manage honors Teams site, shared drive, Constant Contact, and Qualtrics Manage honors space where needed Set agendas and take minutes for the Honors Faculty  Committee and ad hoc committees and groups Monitor enrollments and scheduling for honors, graduation audits, Honors Commencement listing Manage assessment of honors Assist with Honors Academic Standings process and notifications Facilitate and coordinate new and existing processes with other university offices (registrar, marketing and communications, provost’s office, etc.) as needed to impact the success of the University Honors program Perform other duties as assigned Program & Event Planning Plan calendars and coordinate and promote programs and events for incoming and continuing honors students (including but not limited to honors orientation, honors fall welcome, research symposia and travel, and community-building) Work with Admissions on recruiting and yield programs and events Collaborate with other offices as needed to plan events and programs (Career Center, Division of Student Success, business school, alumni affairs, etc.)   Communications Design and update undergraduate program materials and publications, including but not limited to honors program news and promotions, and materials for orientations and admissions events Oversee social media and other communications such as the honors weekly publication and the honors annual report, outreach to honors faculty, annual newsletter Design custom templates as needed using professional-standard design software Liaise with other offices or dean’s office staff to edit honors publications and materials.   Student Management & Mentorship Manage students and student teams, such as honors council and  honors ambassadors Recruit and onboard students Address performance improvement issues if necessary Act as liaison between the director and students, faculty, and administrators Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree with 3+ years of relevant experience; Master’s degree preferred. Experience in a university or college setting. Experience in student services, student mentorship, and/or student outreach. Demonstrated excellent computer and design skills.  Microsoft Office experience, database management, and experience with design software (such as Canva) required. Excellent communication, time management, organizational, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work independently and to assume a leadership role when necessary. Attention to detail, as demonstrated by copyediting and proofreading capabilities. Must be able to handle difficult situations and confidential material with sensitivity and professionalism.    The standard schedule of this position is 8:45 am to 4:45 pm, M-F; however, the ability to keep a flexible schedule to support program objectives, including some evenings and weekends, is necessary.     The salary range for this role is $68,000 - $70,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Associate-Director-of-Student-Leadership-and-Service_R0011005": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Student Leadership and Service",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Associate Director of Student Leadership and Service provides leadership, coordination, and advising for student leadership development, service initiatives, civic engagement, student governance support, and community-based leadership programs. This position serves as a bridge between the Center for Community Engagement and the Office of Student Leadership and Involvement, ensuring that students experience a coordinated and meaningful leadership pathway through service, involvement, governance, and civic engagement. Primary Responsibilities: Journey Leadership Program and Service-Centered Leadership Development Coordinate the day-to-day administration, planning, implementation, and assessment of the Journey Leadership Program under the strategic direction of the Director of the Center for Community Engagement. Develop and implement leadership development experiences rooted in involvement, service, global thinking, civic engagement, and experiential learning. This includes supporting curriculum development, student recruitment, participant communication, leadership workshops, reflection activities, program logistics, and program assessment. Ensure that Journey participants are connected to meaningful opportunities for service, civic engagement, community partnership, and leadership growth. In conjunction with other CCE staff, will provide support for the Alternative Breaks (AB)/International programs. This includes breaks that take place during winter break and spring break. This includes assisting with logistical coordination of the alternative breaks including site selection, partner coordination, student leader training, risk management, travel logistics, reflection activities, and budget management. This also includes developing and leading fundraising efforts in collaboration with University Advancement and other departments to support student participation in service-based travel programs such as AB. Student Government Association and Campus Activities Program Board Advising Serve as the primary staff advisor to the Campus Activities Program Board, providing leadership development, team building, mentorship, and programming support to executive and general board members. Attend board meetings and select evening and weekend events and accompany students to NACA conferences as part of their professional and leadership development. Support the Director of Student Leadership and Involvement in advising the Student Government Association, providing direct guidance to officers, senators, committees, and other student leaders. Assist with leadership development, officer transitions, elections, student advocacy, budget education, policy interpretation, organizational effectiveness, and SGA-sponsored service and civic engagement initiatives. Help student leaders strengthen responsible governance, ethical and inclusive decision-making, communication, and collaboration with University departments. Provide support for student organization recognition, new organization formation, constitution review, funding processes, and compliance with University policies. Supervision, Training, and Campus Partners The Associate Director will assist in the supervision of service-learning student leaders, AB leaders, Journey student leaders and student staff; provide training, support, and performance evaluation. This may include co-supervising community service scholars and graduate fellows. Develop and deliver trainings for student leaders, student organization officers, SGA leaders, service-based organizations, and student program planners. Training areas may include leadership development, ethical service, event planning, risk management, inclusive leadership, community engagement, fiscal responsibility, conflict resolution, and transition planning. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and campus partners to support co-curricular leadership and service initiatives. Serve as a connector between CCE, SLI, student organizations, SGA, and other departments within Student Affairs and across the University. Student Organization Service, Civic Engagement, and Community Impact Initiatives Serve as a primary resource for student organizations interested in service, volunteerism, philanthropy, advocacy, and community impact. Advise service-based student organizations and support student leaders in developing ethical, sustainable, and well-planned service opportunities. Work closely with the Center for Community Engagement, Student Leadership and Involvement, SGA, and other campus partners to plan and execute annual service programs, including campus-wide service days and student-led service initiatives. Assist students with identifying community partners, understanding risk management expectations, planning logistics, and reflecting on the impact of their service. Assessment, Professional Development, and Other duties as assigned Develop and implement assessment tools to measure student learning, program effectiveness, participation, satisfaction, leadership growth, and community impact. Use data to improve the Journey Leadership Program, service initiatives, student leadership trainings, and student governance support. Participate in professional development opportunities, divisional programs, University committees, and Student Affairs initiatives. Assist with major campus events, including Admissions events, Family Weekend, Convocation, Commencement, Recognition Day, Leadership Awards, Orientation, and other programs as assigned. Perform other duties as assigned. Work evenings and weekends as necessary. Requirements/Qualification: Master’s degree in student personnel, counseling, higher education administration or related field is required. Three to five years’ experience working in higher education or a related field. Proven leadership and management skills working with diverse cultures, backgrounds and identities. Strong interpersonal skills and ability to deal effectively with students and staff from diverse backgrounds. Experience advising student organizations, student government, student leaders, service programs, civic engagement initiatives, or leadership development programs is strongly preferred. Demonstrated understanding of student development theory, leadership development pedagogy, experiential learning, civic engagement, and/or service-learning. Experience supervising professional staff. Excellent oral and written skills, as well as listening and advocacy skills. Ability to work with and refer individuals in a crisis. Understanding of philosophy, role, and purpose of a service-learning program. Demonstrated ability to articulate a student development philosophy within higher education. Demonstrated leadership, organization, management, and platform skills; including an ability to oversee a wide variety of activities. Demonstrated experience in program development, implementation, and assessment. Experience and proficiency in the use of technology to include PC use, desktop publishing, Microsoft Office suite, and internet use. Ability to work evenings and weekends on-line, scheduled or on-call     The salary range for this role is $60,000–$75,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Patrol-Officer_R0011004-1": {
      "title": "Patrol Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University is pleased to announce immediate openings as a Patrol Officer with the Police and Public Safety Department. Patrol Officers work in partnership with the Suffolk University community they serve to ensure a safe and secure environment, maintain order, protect students, faculty, staff, and guests, prevent crime, protect buildings and properties, reduce the fear of crime, and improve the overall quality of life for the entire Suffolk University community. Primary/Principal Responsibilities  ·       Provide emergency assistance, perform community relations and conflict resolution. ·       Prevent and deter crime, enforce laws, secure crime scenes, investigate crimes, gather evidence, and take statements in compliance with relevant statutory and constitutional legal requirements. ·       Enforce Suffolk University rules and regulations. ·       Respond to calls for assistance, providing walking escorts to university buildings, banks, MBTA stations, parking garages, or other locations as directed by a supervisor. ·       Administer first aid and CPR to the officer’s appropriate level of training. ·       Respond to alarms, maintain fire watch, and handle emergency evacuations. ·       Interior and exterior patrol of buildings and property by foot, bicycle, or vehicle. ·        Prepare and submit appropriate reports. ·       Provide creditable testimony in court, administrative hearings and/or university proceedings. ·       Operate a department vehicle in emergency and non-emergency situations. ·       Perform other public safety, law enforcement or community policing duties as required. Requirements : Must be at least 21 years of age. Must be a US Citizen or a Naturalized US Citizen. Must possess a high school diploma or have passed the High School Equivalency Training Program (HiSET) which was formally known as the GED. Must be able to obtain a Massachusetts License to Carry Firearms (Class A Large Capacity) in accordance with Massachusetts General Law Chapter 140, Section 131. Must possess and demonstrate ethical behavior, emotional stability, sound judgment, integrity, and ability to accept responsibility for completing assigned duties. Current CPR and First Aid certifications are preferred but not required. Must have no prior felony record. Must possess a valid driver's license or be able to obtain a driver's license prior to hire. Successful completion of a Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC) certified basic Police Recruit Academy (or have an approved MPTC waiver) and ability to obtain and retain Special State Police (SSPO) and Suffolk County Deputy Sheriff powers in accordance with MGL Chapter 22C, section 63. Ability to successfully complete training and show proficiency in the proper use of department issued weapons, police vehicles and equipment. Ability to obtain MA POST Commission Certification and SSPO Warrant of Appointment and pass a state and national background check pursuant to M.G.L. c. 6E, § 4 as a condition of employment. Possess excellent written and oral communication skills; strong ability to write clear and concise reports; the ability to organize and engage in problem solving activities; a strong commitment to prevention and the community policing philosophy. Protect and serve in a diverse community while exercising sound judgement and to take fair and impartial law enforcement actions that are free from bias. Thorough knowledge and understanding of police authority and jurisdiction, constitutional law, and criminal law and procedure. Prior to appointment, selected candidates will be required to pass a comprehensive background investigation, including drug testing and a comprehensive psychological evaluation. Physical Activities/Work Environment   ·       Must be able to sit and stand for extended periods of time; must be able to stoop, kneel, crouch, lift, carry, push, pull, climb and descend stairs, balance, walk, and run as needed. ·       Must be able to access all facilities and areas of the campus on foot, to respond to emergencies or other calls for service, including the upper most floors of campus building when elevators are unavailable. ·       Must be able to physically detain, lift, and push or pull a human being. ·       Must be physically able to operate and communicate using a variety of machines and equipment including radios, telephones, batons, automobiles, office equipment, etc. ·       Must be able to utilize human senses of sight, sound, touch, taste, and smell. ·       Work environment includes exposure to unusual elements, such as severe weather or temperatures and loud noises. ·       Must be able to work flexible hours including rotating shift assignments, nights, weekends, and holidays. ·       Must be available for on-call assignments and overtime shifts as needed. ·       Work environment includes exposure to hazards and physical risks to personal safety. ·       Must be physically fit and able to defend community members and oneself from attack or physical assault. ·       Must be able to walk for extended periods of time. ·       Must be able to wear the required uniform. Special Work Schedule Requirements : May be required to execute duties and responsibilities under life-threatening conditions. The position is considered an essential employee and as such is required to work in preparation for, during and immediately after regional emergencies impacting the university, and / or during emergencies directly impacting the university and/or the region; depending on shift assignment, may be required to work nights and weekends, required to work holidays; at times mandated to work overtime. Police officers are required to successfully complete a structured Field Training Officer (FTO) program and complete training relative to the Massachusetts Police Accreditation Commission (MPAC) process, including training in ethics and integrity; harassment prevention; and the Department's mission, values, and goals, which are provided upon hire, promotion and annually and posted at all police stations on all campuses. The Department is currently hiring for all shifts as well as part-time for the right candidates. We provide all the training and uniforms for this position. What We Offer: 403(b) Retirement Plan (College contributes 6.0% of salary) Comprehensive medical, dental, vision, disability and leave insurance Flexible Spending Account (FSA/HSA) Employee Assistance Program Tuition Benefits (100% undergraduate tuition remission for employees, and their dependents)     The salary range for this role is Salary Range ($28.00-$40.00 hourly plus differential, depending on experience).     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Director-and-Professor-of-Legal-Writing_R0010957": {
      "title": "Director and Professor of Legal Writing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University Law School is searching for a Professor of Legal Writing to join our faculty and serve as the D irector of Legal Practice Skills (LPS) , our top-five tenure-track legal writing program , starting in the 2027-2028 academic year . We welcome applications from individuals with substantial experience teaching in legal writing programs , a record of scholarship, significant service experience, and leadership experience or potential , including those with tenure , long-term contracts , or other similar record of accomplishments .  We are looking for an innovative, collaborative leader who will maintain the program’s excellence and lead its continued evolution in a time of rapid change in legal practice and technology .     The Director of LPS will teach one section of the year-long first-year legal writing course and devote significant time to leading the program , including:    overseeing legal writing adjuncts and visitors;    liaising with the administration on matters related to LPS;    mentoring new legal writing faculty;    planning and overseeing curricular innovation;    managing internal and external communications;    participating and leading in the national legal writing community;    conducting program review and strategic planning;    maintaining involvement in regional and national legal writing organizations and initiatives;    chairing the LPS faculty committee.   Support for scholarship, research, and professional development will be provided. T enure-track and tenured legal writing professors participate fully in faculty governance with the same voting rights as all tenure-track and tenured faculty.    Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae or resume, references, and a cover letter. Cover letters should be addressed to Professors Rosa Kim ( rkim@suffolk.edu ) and Rebecca Curtin ( rcurtin@suffolk.edu ), Co-Chairs of the Legal Practice Skills Committee, and all materials must be uploaded to Suffolk’s job posting website . We will be reviewing ca ndidates on a rolling basis and encourage early inquiries and applications.        The salary range for this role is $140,000 - $160,000 with the opportunity for additional compensation for administrative duties.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Tenure-Track-Faculty-Positions-in-Civil-Procedure--Property--and-Torts_R0010944": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Positions in Civil Procedure, Property, and Torts",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University Law School in Boston seeks to fill up to three entry-level, tenure-track faculty positions starting in 2027-2028.  Candidates should have strong academic and professional backgrounds and show promise of high achievement in scholarship, teaching, and service. We welcome future colleagues who would contribute to any of our faculty’s broad research perspectives, including quantitative or qualitative empiricism; critical, historical, comparative, or philosophical approaches; and doctrinal or policy analysis. Our search will focus on candidates with a primary interest in Civil Procedure, Property, and/or Torts, but we will also consider a candidate’s potential to contribute to our needs in other areas, including Bankruptcy, Commercial Law, Family Law, Fintech Law, Labor & Employment Law, and our Concentrations.  Although we will be considering candidates through the AALS Faculty Appointments Register, we encourage candidates with a particular interest in Suffolk Law to submit a direct application through our hiring website as soon as possible, as we will begin reviewing applications immediately. Candidates should include in their applications a curriculum vitae, research agenda, and a letter of interest addressed to Professors Erin Braatz and Linda Simard, Co-Chairs of the Appointments Committee.     The salary range for this role is $130,000 to $145,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Director--Graduate-Admission-Systems_R0010990": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Graduate Admission Systems",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 24 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSuffolk University's Office of Graduate Admission relies on two enterprise systems - Slate CRM and Workday Student - to manage the full prospect-to-enrollment lifecycle for the College of Arts & Sciences (CAS) and the Sawyer Business School (SBS). The Assistant Director, Graduate Admission Systems is the office's dedicated technical owner of these platforms: the primary Slate administrator and the office's point person for Workday Student configuration, data integrity, and integrations. The Assistant Director, Graduate Admission Systems owns the technical infrastructure staff work within - rules, workflows, forms, imports, integrations, document management, and reporting - and partners closely with University IT, Office of Institutional Research, the Registrar, Financial Aid, International Student Services, INTO Suffolk, and Slate/Workday vendor resources to keep both systems accurate, current, and aligned to the office's recruitment and enrollment strategy. This role is well suited to a systems-minded admission or enrollment professional who wants to specialize technically and who can operate independently as the subject-matter expert on both platforms. Responsibilities Slate CRM Administration & Configuration Serve as primary Slate administrator for the Office of Graduate Admission: owns rules and updates them as programs, terms, and policies change. Build and maintain portals, forms (RFI, application, application-review), and event templates. Manage the annual cycle refresh (rounds, retention policy maintenance, Grad Source Format checks). Own configuration for workflows, including source origins that fall out of the typical graduate degree admission review.) Manage application-change workflows (and troubleshoot day-to-day Slate issues escalated by the admission and operations teams. Manage source formats for the graduate programs, including vendor imports and operational types. Provide Financial Aid Office support for decision validation and reporting needs drawn from Slate. Reconcile data between Slate and the INTO Suffolk application system (Salesforce) and manage the flow of applications between the two systems; serve as the technical point of contact for changes to programs or admission requirements communicated to INTO. Develop and implement a data-integrity testing strategy for Slate, distinct from routine QA reporting, to catch configuration and data errors before they reach production. Maintain internal Slate documentation so configuration decisions and standard operating procedures are preserved beyond any one person's institutional knowledge. Integrate CRM document management with Perceptive ImageNow, ensuring application documents and records are captured, indexed, and accessible in accordance with University document-management standards. Workday Student Administration & Data Integrity Serve as the office's primary point of contact for Workday Student functions relevant to graduate admission: application groupings, user provisioning, workflow and business-process updates, and end-of-cycle wrap-up. Manage Workday import processes, including ISSO/SEVIS data loads, ensuring accurate data handling during peak import periods. Resolve duplicate records and other data-integrity issues in partnership with Admissions Operations, University IT and the Registrar's Office; run and review QA reports on a recurring basis. Maintain awareness of Workday integration schedules and escalate service needs (e.g., integration updates) to University IT as required. Identify and document a backup/succession plan for Workday permissions specific to this role, so graduate admission is never a single point of failure on Workday access. Reporting, Documentation & Process Improvement Build and maintain ad hoc and recurring Slate and Workday reports for the Senior VP of Enrollment Management, Director, Associate Director, and operations team, including pre-building standard reports ahead of each admission cycle. Partner with the Director to develop a core library of queries/reports that reduces day-to-day dependence on this role for routine data requests. Document Slate and Workday procedures and keep documentation current as configuration changes. Recommend and implement process improvements that reduce manual work for the admission and operations teams, replacing manual steps with system-driven solutions where feasible. Training & Office Contributions Train Graduate Admission staff on Slate and Workday functionality relevant to their roles; serve as a resource for system questions. Participate in office-wide systems initiatives and cross-departmental projects (e.g., Financial Aid, International Student Services, Registrar) as assigned by the Director. Support and staff marquis admission events as needed. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution, or equivalent combination of education and experience. 5–7 years of experience administering a CRM (Slate strongly preferred) and/or a student information system in a higher education setting. Direct, hands-on experience configuring Slate: rules, workflows, forms, portals, and communication/event modules. Working technical proficiency to troubleshoot and resolve complex data and configuration issues. Experience with Workday Student or a comparable SIS (e.g., Banner, PeopleSoft), including data imports, integrations, and reporting. Demonstrated ability to translate operational needs from non-technical staff into system configuration and solutions. Strong data-integrity discipline: experience with duplicate resolution, QA reporting, and audit processes. Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to produce clear documentation and training materials for non-technical users. Ability to work independently as a subject-matter expert while collaborating across admission, IT, financial aid, and registrar functions. This position may require travel as well as evening and weekend work. Preferred Experience specific to graduate admission cycles, merit/financial aid workflows, or international student processes (SEVIS/ISSO). Slate certification(s) or Workday Student certification/Pro credential. Experience serving as a functional liaison between an admission office and University IT or a vendor support team. Familiarity with SEVIS/ISSO reporting requirements and international partnership integrations (e.g., INTO). Experience with a document management system (e.g., Perceptive ImageNow) integrated with a CRM or SIS.     The salary range for this role is $69,000 - $83,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Assistant-Director-of-Academic-Operations_R0010955": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Academic Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-04\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Assistant Director of Academic Operations provides comprehensive administrative and operational leadership for doctoral program administration within the College of Arts & Sciences, with primary responsibility for admissions, accreditation, student records, and program coordination. The role collaborates closely with faculty and academic leadership to support clinical training processes, student progression, and community engagement. In addition, the Assistant Director supports faculty governance operations, including committee coordination and documentation, and ensures alignment of key academic materials such as the faculty manual and academic catalog with approved governance actions. The position also oversees college-level web content management, contributes to event planning and student engagement initiatives, and manages select administrative functions, including faculty funding processes and internal communications. This role requires strong organizational, communication, and project management skills, as well as the ability to manage complex, cyclical academic processes. Primary Responsibilities: Doctoral Program Support Collaborate closely with the Director of Clinical Training (DCT), Assistant Director of Clinical Training (ADCT), Clinical Training Coordinator (CTC), and Administrative Services Manager (ASM) to support the administration of the doctoral program. Manage and execute the full admissions cycle in partnership with the Office of Graduate Admissions, including application review logistics, faculty assignment, Slate processing, interview weekend coordination, and oversight of waitlist and offer processes. Support APA accreditation processes, including preparation of annual reports and self-studies, coordination of site visit logistics, and maintenance of accurate records and web content. Maintain and manage student records, program data, and tracking systems to ensure accuracy and compliance. Coordinate orientation and onboarding for incoming doctoral cohorts. Develop and manage communication systems for current students, faculty, and alumni to foster engagement and streamline information sharing. Support doctoral program operations, including research milestones (e.g., proposals, dissertations), clinical training placements (practica and internships), annual student evaluations, and program communications (shared inbox management). Faculty Governance Support Provide administrative support for faculty governance bodies, including scheduling, room reservations, agenda development, and preparation of meeting materials. Serve as recorder for standing committees, including the Graduate Curriculum Committee and Educational Policy Committee; prepare agendas and publish meeting minutes. Provide support as needed for additional committees, including the Undergraduate Academic Shared Committee and Undergraduate Curriculum Committee. Support CAS Faculty Assembly operations, including agenda preparation and documentation of proceedings. Content & Web Management Serve as primary point of contact for College of Arts & Sciences (CAS) web content; ensure accuracy and make updates within the CMS. Support the annual revision of the CAS academic catalog, including direct CMS updates. Coordinate the editing and publication of the CAS Faculty Manual, ensuring alignment with actions and policies approved through faculty governance processes. Assist in maintaining internal platforms, including CAS Faculty Teams sites and Canvas workspaces. Coordinate with academic departments and the Office of Marketing & Communications to update and align marketing materials with official academic content. Events & Program Support Support planning and execution of CAS events as needed, including commencement, faculty recognition events, and faculty retreats. Assist with onboarding, orientation planning, and student engagement initiatives. Support admissions and recruitment events through year-round planning, logistics coordination, and cross-campus collaboration. General Administrative Support Administer faculty funding processes, including coordinating application cycles, tracking submissions, and supporting review and disbursement workflows. Monitor and manage the Dean’s Office shared email account, ensuring timely responses, appropriate triage, and escalation as needed. Maintain and update departmental and college listservs to support effective internal communication. Support Teaching Assistant (TA) and Research Assistant (RA) programs, including application coordination and tracking. Provide administrative support for First-Year Seminar and cross-college programs, including course entry, schedule updates, and coordination of experiential learning activities (e.g., field trips, guest speakers). Additional duties may be assigned as needed to support departmental and college priorities. Requirements Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred At least 3+ years of relevant professional or administrative experience, preferably in higher education Demonstrated ability to manage complex, multi-phase projects (e.g., admissions cycles, accreditation, or governance processes) with a high degree of independence Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills, with the ability to develop and improve administrative systems and workflows Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including experience drafting, editing, and managing official or policy-related documents Proven ability to build and maintain effective working relationships across teams and organizational levels Experience handling sensitive or confidential information with discretion Proficiency with Microsoft Office and ability to learn new technologies quickly Must be able to occasionally work evenings and weekends as needed for events     The salary range for this role is $65,000-$69,000.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Senior-Director--Advancement-Services_R0010938": {
      "title": "Senior Director, Advancement Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-27\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nReporting to the Associate VP, Development Operations, the Senior Director of Advancement Services (SDAS) is responsible for the management, administration and integrity of the information in the fundraising database, Raiser’s Edge NXT, and has a critical leadership role in a wide variety of Advancement areas including, but not limited to, the core database and modules, reporting and analytics, training, managing/enhancing tertiary systems and connectivity, customer support, workflow, data and account setup and audits, database rights and security, business needs analyses, data mining, special projects, and policies and procedures.  S/he will oversee gift processing, receipting and acknowledging, management of biographical, professional and prospect-related data.  S/he will be responsible for serving all of the schools and central administration regarding their information needs for fundraising and alumni communications.  As needed, s/he will represent Advancement by serving on University committees and task forces, especially in regards to matters involving data and technology.  The SDAS works closely with Suffolk University colleagues across the campus including, but not limited to, Finance, the Center for Career Equity, Development, and Success, ITS, Office of Marketing and Communications, Registrar, Public Affairs, the three Schools and various academic offices and departments. Primary Responsibilities: Serves as the most senior manager of the function and the staff comprising the Advancement Services team Manages a team of five FTE staff: two Associate Directors, a Senior Gift and Reporting Specialist, one Data Coordinator and student workers Principal database administrator for the 150,000 record Raiser’s Edge NXT database, the University’s alumni and fundraising database management system. Responsible for data entry and data appends, gift processing and acknowledgments, list and report production, data mining, customer support, data and account set-up and audits, policies and procedures, goal setting/benchmarking and system integrity and functioning. Serve as Advancement’s administrative and technical lead and the development of new business processes in Raiser’s Edge NXT. Participates in national Advancement Services and application user groups and listservs to keep abreast of developing technologies and best practices Lead Advancement Services Liaison to ITS, Finance, Registrar, Institutional Research, Public Affairs and the Center for Career Equity, Development, and Success Responsible for serving all of the schools and central administration with their information needs for fundraising and alumni communication. Engages in monthly and regular reconciliation meetings and discussions with Finance Evaluate, provide guidance, and edit fund creations for most gift agreements and amendments. Partners with ITS office for various technical database administrative and substantive enhancement initiatives.  Manages and perform tasks that establish and enhance data integrity Establish standards for, and monitor the integrity of, the information contained in Advancement’s fundraising database system and help determine how to translate data into usable, actionable information for the formation and execution of fundraising strategies. Lead and manage the Advancement Services department with the goal of increasing the quantity and quality of data maintained while constantly striving to achieve new efficiencies Responsible for core database and modules’ data integrity, user accounts, rights and security audits Perform regular audits of system data including demographic, prospect management and fundraising–if necessary add additional/modified procedures and manage training; determine appropriate placement of data, coding, mapping and uploading working in conjunction with Advancement,  ITS and (other University colleagues in the case of CORE data) Leads effort to create, analyze and distribute lists and reports to support fundraising initiatives and alumni outreach. Responsible for creating, generating and improving queries, exports, lists and reports for use by senior management, Advancement staff, treasurer and business office colleagues, external constituencies, including trustee and development committees as requested.  Export, shape and analysis of a broad diversity of Raiser’s Edge data including gift and pledge transactions, demographic, prospect management; and activities/events information Coordinate with Advancement team to ensure that all staff have access to necessary database information and tools and how to best use our data to fulfill their various business needs Manage use of tertiary systems to extract a maximum amount of data from the ADV database Lead efforts and work closely with Advancement colleagues and vendors on all data issues and enhancements related to tertiary systems, including, but not limited to Omatic, Runner Technologies, Iwave, and Accudata. Responsible for creating and implementing Policies, Procedures, Audits, Industry Standards and Office Training In conjunction with the Assoc. VP and  VP for Advancement, design policies for system use that meet CAE, IRS regulations and the Advancement Office’s business needs Continually assess and manage Advancement Services’ workflow, customer support operations, and policies and procedures Monitor user compliance, IRS regulations and make recommendations as necessary. Take initiative and lead information management projects to ensure access to Raiser’s Edge NXT data and demonstrate strong proactive communication regarding data and database issues Manage the training of Advancement staff on basic report generation and perform more complex reporting requests and personally manages all requests.  Train and mentor other Advancement Services staff on all report-related responsibilities Requirements/Qualification: Bachelor’s degree plus additional technical training in systems and fundraising protocol Master’s Degree in a related field desirable At least eight (8) years of progressive Advancement database systems management experience Desirable qualifications include experience with complex relational databases that serve the higher education fundraising environment, thorough knowledge of the Raiser’s Edge fundraising database, and proficiency using Tableau and other data visualization tools. Advanced level of working knowledge and understanding of fundraising policies and guidelines including IRS, CASE and CAE reporting guidelines Advanced computer skills including designing complex queries and user-friendly exports and reports and proficiency with MS Office applications. Demonstrated ability to develop training materials, teach and administer policy guidelines Ability to develop and document reporting procedures and policies. Demonstrated management and leadership experience in a complex organization Excellent written and verbal communication skills, a great commitment to customer service and the ability to interact with executive level users and a wide variety of communication/work styles Ability to review and edit gift and fund agreements and amendments. Flexibility initiating and responding to changing priorities and situations. Demonstrated ability to interact with both internal and external constituents with demonstrated integrity and a spirit of enthusiasm for the University. Understanding of and appreciation for the mission and goals of the University. Demonstrated experience working with diverse populations and a strong commitment to help developing and implementing the University’s Diversity, Equity and Inclusion policies and goals A high level of diplomacy, tact and confidentiality. Ability to work weekends and evenings as assigned. Eagerness to perform additional duties as assigned and assist with special projects as assigned.     The salary range for this role is $90K-$150K.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Stewardship-and-Donor-Relations_R0010937": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Stewardship and Donor Relations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-22\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Assistant Director of Stewardship and Donor Relations works with the Assistant Vice President of Alumni Engagement and Donor Relations to develop and implement a comprehensive stewardship and donor relations program that maintains consistent engagement with donors through a variety of events, communications, and personal outreach. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Alumni Engagement and Donor Relations works closely with colleagues across the Advancement function to cultivate and build donor relationships and express the University’s gratitude to donors, ensuring that they are appreciated, informed, and engaged. This position plays a key role in leading a stewardship program that successfully communicates to donors at all levels the transformational impact of their support, including assisting with personalized stewardship initiatives for principal and major gift donors. This position serves as project manager for stewardship activities including reports, publications, and a variety of correspondence and video engagement. This position also leads or assists with diverse alumni and donor engagement events and other institutional events, from major donor recognition events and naming dedications to small gatherings with University and Advancement leadership to Commencement and Reunion activities.  Specific Duties & Responsibilities: •     Works directly with frontline fundraisers to implement stewardship programs for donors and prospects associated with principal gifts ($1M and above) and major gifts ($50k and above) •    Works with colleagues including the Sr. Director of Advancement Communications to manage the development and creation of donor impact reports, major gift presentations and reports, and other print materials related to donor recognition •    Crafts personalized Thankview videos for major and principal gift donors •    Assists in the planning and implementation of a variety of special stewardship and donor recognition events •    Communicates with donors and builds relationships in support of their stewardship and special initiatives •    In conjunction with other Advancement colleagues, assists with the student scholarship recipient thank you program and annual scholarship fund reporting to all donors with endowed and term funds •    Coordinates other stewardship initiatives as identified such as donor photo albums and framed photos and memorabilia, and a wide range of donor recognition gifts for principal and major gift donors in particular •   Assists with the process related to donor gift acknowledgement letters and related research and communications as required, particularly at the Summa level of $1K+ •   Assists in developing donor relations strategy for Suffolk University   Job Requirements Bachelor’s degree or equivalent required 2-3 years of progressive experience in a development or nonprofit setting Experience managing special events  Strong interpersonal and communication skills being Proven project management, communication, and relationship building experience Ability to work independently or in a group setting, interacting with people at all levels of the University and with a variety of campus partners Ability to respond quickly to changing University needs and priorities Ability to handle multiple projects simultaneously in a fast-paced environment The Division of Advancement operates on a hybrid schedule, with three days per week in the office and two remote, to ensure a consistent campus presence and support collaboration. Suffolk values in-person engagement, which strengthens relationships across Advancement and the broader campus community and supports effective planning and execution of events and initiatives. Additional in-office presence may be required based on business needs, event schedules, and operational priorities. On remote days, it is expected for employees to be fully available during regular working hours and responsive to colleagues, volunteers, and campus partners. In-office days may occasionally shift to accommodate event preparation and key responsibilities     The salary range for this role is $46,500.00 to $77,500.00.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
    },
    "https://suffolk.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Boston-MA/Professional-STEM-Tutor-1_R0010301-1": {
      "title": "Professional STEM Tutor-1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-16\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\n% of Time Responsibility 75 Tutor students who request STEM assistance: individual to small group sessions in math/stats drop-in, one-on-one appointments in STEM disciplines. Tutoring may be online or in the center. Tutors will write notes in our tutor management system for individual appointments. 10 During open appointment blocks, perform various academic support tasks according to tutor’s skills and interests as assigned by CLAS management. 5 Attend training on specific topics related to assisting college students with STEM subjects, assisting students of concern, etc.  Training may be in person or online. Meet with program supervisors to discuss other issues. 5 Facilitate large group tutoring sessions or exam review sessions. 5 Write notes for all tutoring sessions in appointment tracking system (EAB Navigate). Requirements/Qualifications: Master's Degree or higher in a STEM discipline or related area Knowledge of successful strategies for tutoring a diverse college student population Demonstrated commitment to justice, equity, diversity, and inclusion (especially inclusion of groups historically excluded from STEM fields) as well as anti-racist tutoring praxis Excellent written and oral communication skills Ability to work in person on Suffolk’s downtown Boston campus Proven ability to work independently and as part of a team Creativity and flexibility in tutoring Experience with EAB Navigate, TutorTrac, WCOnline, or other appointment management platforms a plus     The salary range for this role is $40 per hour.     Salary Transparency Suffolk University provides salary ranges as a good faith estimate of what it reasonably expects to pay for a role at the time the position is posted. The actual salary offered to the selected candidate will depend on various factors, including, but not limited to, the position’s scope and responsibilities, the candidate’s experience, education, training, and applicable legal requirements. The listed pay range reflects base salary only and does not include additional benefits or compensation. Equal Opportunity Statement Suffolk University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ethnicity, religion, national origin, sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, age, disability, military status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. We welcome applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds, perspectives, and experiences. The University strives to recruit, develop and retain faculty and staff committed to educational excellence, the robust exchange of ideas, and engage in membership in a diverse and inclusive community."
